# Emergent Intelligence

Emergent intelligence refers to the phenomenon where intelligent behavior arises from complex systems without any single component or central controller directing it. This concept, explored by Kevin Kelly in [[Out of Control - Kevin Kelly]], shows that intelligence can be a property of distributed networks, self-organizing systems, and collective processes.

## Key Ideas

- Intelligence is not limited to individual minds—it can emerge from interactions between many simple agents.
- Decentralized systems often exhibit greater adaptability and resilience than centralized ones.
- Feedback loops, self-organization, and distributed decision-making produce forms of intelligence that cannot be reduced to any single component.

## Connection to Collective Intelligence

Emergent intelligence connects to James Surowiecki's [[The Wisdom of Crowds - James Surowiecki]], where collective intelligence arises from aggregating diverse opinions. Both perspectives show that intelligence operates at the system level, not just the individual level.

